# GOLF/2 tool text library — byte ranges, compare/find, decimal and hex.
# Authored in code-page mnemonic form, like lib/prelude.golfj, and prepended to
# gtools/ programs by gtools/build (prelude, then lib/t*.golfj in glob order,
# then the program).  It is NOT linked into ordinary user programs.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHAT A "TEXT BUFFER" IS HERE — the decision every word below is built on
# ===========================================================================
#
# A text buffer in this library is a **(addr, len) pair on the data stack**: a
# raw start address and a byte count, in that order, unadorned by any header.
# `n` and `q` take one; `p` and `r` take two, haystack then needle.  Nothing
# here ever allocates, copies or NUL-terminates on your behalf.
#
# The repo already has a competing convention — the `“…“` block, `[len:4][raw
# bytes]`, whose value is the address of the 4-byte length field, which `O`
# (puts) and `U` (chars) speak.  It was rejected as this library's currency for
# three reasons:
#
#   1. A wave-3 tool gets its bytes from `read(2)`.  The kernel hands back a
#      buffer address it was given and a count it returns — a (addr, len) pair,
#      exactly.  Adopting the block form would mean either reserving four bytes
#      in front of every buffer and storing the count into them before any word
#      here could look at it, or copying the whole file.  Adopting the pair form
#      costs nothing: the two values are already on the stack.
#   2. The pair form SUBSUMES the block form.  A block `t` becomes a pair on the
#      spot — `←t 4﹢` is the address, `←t @` is the length (a 32-bit `@`,
#      because a string's length prefix is still four bytes and W4A left it
#      alone).  The reverse direction is a copy.  So a caller can always feed a
#      literal to these words; a block-based library could never have been fed
#      a slice of a slurped file without one.
#   3. A pair is the only form that can name a SUBRANGE.  Every tool here walks
#      a buffer: "the rest of the line from the cursor", "the field between two
#      tabs".  With a pair those are `buf i ﹢` and a subtracted length, free.
#      There is no way to make a `“…“` block point into the middle of another.
#
# The price is that a range is unnamed and unchecked — two stack cells that
# nothing ties together, and no word here bounds-checks against a buffer end it
# cannot see.  Every word that is GIVEN a length therefore treats it as gospel
# and reads at most that many bytes; `n` in particular stops at `len` even with
# no terminator in sight, which is what makes it safe to call on the last number
# in a file.  The three words with no length argument — `l` (always two bytes),
# `m` (told how many) and `o` (as many as the number needs, and `o` WRITES) —
# say so in their own comments, and are the three places a caller has to think.
#
# The pair interoperates with lib/tio.golfj by construction and WITHOUT any
# dependency on it: tio slurps a file and yields (addr, len); these words take
# (addr, len).  Nothing below calls a tio word — `ttext` sorts after `tio` and
# before `tutf` in gtools/build's glob, so `tutf` is out of reach by definition
# and `tio` is out of reach by choice.  The only prelude words used below are
# `X` and `Y`; not even `⍶` alloc, since nothing here allocates.
#
# ===========================================================================
# Words.  Ten lowercase letters, `i`–`r`, per ../REGISTRY.md §2.1 — and that is
# the WHOLE pool this library owns, so nothing below is spent on a word that is
# two tokens of arithmetic away from another one.
# ===========================================================================
#   i  ch -> f              is-digit: 0 for '0'-'9', 1 otherwise
#   j  ch -> f              is-ASCII-letter: 0 for A-Z a-z, 1 otherwise
#   k  ch -> v              hex digit value 0..15, or -1 if not a hex digit
#   l  addr -> b            the two hex digits at addr as a byte 0..255, or -1
#   m  src dst n ->         copy n bytes from src to dst (forward; no overlap)
#   n  addr len -> v used   parse an unsigned decimal run: value, bytes consumed
#   o  v addr -> nb         write v as unsigned decimal at addr; bytes written
#   p  a alen b blen -> f   0 iff the range (a,alen) starts with (b,blen)
#   q  addr len ch -> i     index of the first ch in the range, or -1
#   r  a alen b blen -> i   index of the first (b,blen) in (a,alen), or -1
#
# THE THREE CLASSIFIERS ARE `i`, `j` AND `k 0≺`.  is-hex-digit is not a word of
# its own: `k` already computes it, so the test is `<ch> k 0≺` — negative means
# "not a hex digit", so `≺` against 0 yields 0 exactly for a hex digit, in the
# same zero-is-true polarity as `i` and `j`.  Use `" 0≺` instead when you want
# to keep the value.  A whole letter whose body would be `k 0≺` is not a trade
# worth making out of ten.
#
# COMPARE AND STARTS-WITH ARE ONE WORD.  `p a alen b blen` reads both ways:
# "the range at a begins with the blen bytes at b" and, when alen == blen,
# "these two ranges are equal".  Two ranges of the same length are equal exactly
# when either starts with the other, so equality is `<a> <n> <b> <n> p`.  The
# four-argument form is what makes it safe in the encoder's inner loop: a needle
# longer than what is left of the haystack yields 1 (no match) instead of
# reading past the end, which a three-argument `(a, b, n)` could not express.
# Against a `“…“` literal `t` the call is `<a> <alen> ←t 4﹢ ←t @ p`.
#
# FLAG POLARITY IS ZERO-IS-TRUE, like the prelude's `T` and like `[` itself:
# every `f` above is 0 for yes and 1 for no, so `<ch> j [ … ]` runs the block on
# a letter with no adapter in between.  The -1/0 flags that `≡ ﹤ ≺` produce are
# converted with `1﹢` where they are used; they never leak out of a word.
#
# NOT-FOUND IS -1, not a length or a zero.  `q` and `r` return an INDEX, and 0
# is a perfectly good index (the needle at the very start), so the marker has to
# live outside the index range; `k` and `l` return a VALUE, and 0 is the value
# of '0'/"00", so the same applies.  -1 is `1±` here, since GOLF literals are
# non-negative.  Test it with `0≺` (signed!) and never with `»`/`﹤`, which are
# unsigned and see -1 as the largest number there is.
#
#   *** DO NOT PARK A -1 IN A →x/←x VARIABLE. ***  The variable bank is FOUR
#   bytes per name and `←x` compiles to `mov eax,[cell]`, which ZERO-extends:
#   `q` -> -1 -> `→x` -> `←x` gives 4294967295, and `0≺` on that is false, so
#   the not-found branch silently does not run.  (A loop bound computed from it
#   then runs about four billion times, which is how this was found.)  Test the
#   marker while it is still on the DATA stack — `" 0≺ 1﹢ [ _ <fallback> ] →x`
#   is the shape — or keep the whole value on the stack.  This is the same
#   4-byte hazard ../REGISTRY.md §3 warns about for pointers above 2^31; it
#   just bites at the other end of the range.  Nothing in this library stores
#   anything in a variable, so every -1 below is a true 64-bit -1 when it is
#   handed back.
#
# ===========================================================================
# ASCII-ONLY LETTERS — a deliberate divergence from tools/codepage.py
# ===========================================================================
# Wave 3 ports codepage.py's `encode`, whose mnemonic scan is driven by Python's
# `str.isalpha()`.  That is UNICODE-aware: it is true for 'é', 'Ω', 'ᵃ' and some
# 130 000 other code points.  `j` matches ASCII A-Z a-z and nothing else — it
# ORs in bit 5 to fold case and then tests the single range 'a'..'z', so every
# byte >= 0x80 (including every UTF-8 continuation byte) classifies as not a
# letter.
#
# The two agree on all real input and that is not luck: `encode` only consults
# isalpha() on the run immediately after a `\`, i.e. on a mnemonic name, and
# every mnemonic in ../REGISTRY.md §1 is ASCII by the rule that mnemonics are
# `\word` escapes typed on an ASCII keyboard.  They would diverge on a source
# file containing `\café`, which Python would read as the four-letter run
# "café" and this library as "caf" followed by a stray byte.  Written down here
# rather than left for someone to rediscover; if a non-ASCII mnemonic is ever
# allocated, `j` is the word that has to grow a UTF-8 decoder.
#
# ===========================================================================
# Memory.  This library allocates NO new fixed address and NO variable name.
# ===========================================================================
# Every word here that touches scratch at all opens with `X` and closes with
# `Y`, and then uses the prelude's own bank `s0`–`s7` (0x4F0060, stride 8 —
# 5177440 5177448
# 5177456 5177464 5177472 5177480 5177488 5177496).  That is what the spill
# frame is for: `X` copies all eight cells to the spill stack, so inside the
# frame they are private, and `Y` puts the caller's back.  Consequences worth
# stating:
#
#   * These words nest.  `r` calls `p` in its inner loop and `p` clobbers every
#     scratch cell; because `p` frames, `r`'s own state survives.  A tool may
#     equally call any of these from inside a `€` map or a `⇤` fold.
#   * No collision with a gtools/ program is possible.  ../REGISTRY.md §2.1 does
#     permit a tool library to use `→x`/`←x`, and this one declines: the
#     variable bank has ONE global cell per name, so a reserved-name list would
#     be a rule wave 3 has to remember, whereas a spill frame is a rule the
#     machine enforces.  **Variables allocated by ttext: none.**
#   * No truncation is possible either.  The bank is 64-bit and read with `⊙`;
#     the variable bank is four bytes per name, which would have quietly cut a
#     pointer above 2^31 in half.  Nothing here is near that today (the heap
#     starts at 0x500000) but `⎈`-facing code has no business assuming it.
#   * `l` and `k` are the two words a hot hex loop calls per byte.  `k` is a
#     leaf — no scratch, no frame — so it is callable from anywhere, including
#     from inside another word's live scratch.  `l` does frame, because it must
#     hold two digit values across a call to `k`.
#
# ===========================================================================
# House rules inherited from the prelude — see its header for the reasoning.
# ===========================================================================
#   * Raw atoms only.  Every pointer, index and flag step below is `﹢ ﹣ ﹡ ⊓ ⊔
#     ﹤` (\radd \rsub \rmul \rmin \rmax \rlt), never the bare ASCII `+ - * ⌈ ⌊`:
#     M-VEC made those five polymorphic and the dispatcher would recurse through
#     this library's own address math.  `/` `%` `|` `≡` `≺` `?` `,` are NOT in
#     the hook table and stay bare; `o`'s digit extraction is `10/` and `10%`
#     on purpose, because those are UNSIGNED and `÷`/`∣` are not.  `Ṅ` uses the
#     same unsigned pair but opens with a `0≺` sign test and so renders 2^64-1
#     as "-1"; `o` never looks at the top bit, which is why it can print it.
#   * `{…}` is a DO-while: the body always runs once.  Every loop that can have
#     zero iterations is guarded with `<n> 0≡ [ {…} ]` — `≡` yields -1 when the
#     count is 0, so `[` (which runs on TOS==0) skips.  The cases that guard
#     buys: an empty range (`q`/`n` with len 0), an empty needle (`p` with
#     blen 0, which is a match at every position, so `r` returns 0), and a
#     zero-length copy (`m`).  `o`'s emit loop is deliberately UNGUARDED — its
#     digit count is at least 1 even for the value 0, which is the one case a
#     naive "while v != 0" formatter gets wrong.
#   * Byte 0x5C (swap) is never emitted: `decode` renders it as a bare `\`,
#     which `encode` would then read as the escape for whatever follows.  There
#     is no swap below; where an operand order needed reversing it went through
#     scratch instead.  See prelude.golfj:207-213.
#   * `^` (early return) is used inside `[…]` in `k` only, in the style of
#     self/golf2.golfj's `t`; every other word has a single exit so that its `Y`
#     is unmistakably paired with its `X`.
#
# ===========================================================================

# i : is-digit.  (ch - '0') <u 10 is -1 for a digit; ﹢1 turns that into the
# zero-is-true flag.  A leaf, and the inner test of `n`.
:i48\rsub 10\rlt 1\radd;

# j : is-ASCII-letter, A-Z a-z ONLY (see the divergence note above).  `|32` sets
# bit 5, which maps 'A'-'Z' onto 'a'-'z' and leaves 'a'-'z' alone, so one range
# test covers both cases.  Digits survive it unchanged (they already have bit 5)
# and land far below 'a'; '@' '[' '`' '{' and every byte >= 0x80 land outside.
:j32\or 97\rsub 26\rlt 1\radd;

# k : hex digit -> 0..15, or -1.  Digits first (the common case in a hex dump),
# then the letters, folded to lower case by the same `|32` trick: 'a'..'f' is
# the 6-wide range at 97, and 'a'-87 == 10.  Leaf: no scratch, no frame, safe to
# call with someone else's s0..s7 live.  `<ch> k 0≺` is the is-hex-digit test.
:k"i[48\rsub^]"32\or 97\rsub 6\rlt 1\radd[32\or 87\rsub^]_1\neg;

# l : the two hex digits at addr as one byte, or -1 if either is not a hex
# digit.  s0 holds the high nibble across the second `k` call and s1 the low one
# — hence the frame.  The -1 is pushed FIRST and dropped only when both nibbles
# check out, so the two guards need no else-branch and the word has one exit.
# THIS IS THE ONE WORD HERE WITHOUT A LENGTH ARGUMENT: it reads addr and addr+1
# unconditionally, so a caller sweeping to the end of a buffer must stop one
# byte short of it (or, in `data/blob.hex`'s fixed "XX XX" layout, step by 3).
:l X"?k 5177440\store 1\radd?k 5177448\store 1\neg 5177440\fetch 0\slt[5177448\fetch 0\slt[_5177440\fetch 16\rmul 5177448\fetch\radd]]Y;

# m : copy n bytes src -> dst, ascending.  s0 src · s1 dst · s2 n · s3 index.
# Guarded, so n == 0 copies nothing.  Ascending order means it is safe when dst
# is below src and NOT when the two overlap the other way; no tool here does.
:m X5177456\store 5177448\store 5177440\store 0 5177464\store 5177456\fetch 0\eq[{5177440\fetch 5177464\fetch\radd?5177448\fetch 5177464\fetch\radd,5177464\fetch 1\radd 5177464\store 5177464\fetch 5177456\fetch\rlt 1\radd}]Y;

# n : parse an unsigned decimal run at (addr,len) -> value, bytes consumed.
# Returning the count is the point: `data/blob.hex`'s byte counts and the `` ` ``
# escape's length are followed by a delimiter the caller must then step over,
# and it cannot know how many digits it just passed.  No digits at all is
# (0, 0), which is how a caller detects "that was not a number" — and is why the
# value alone would have been a useless return.
# s0 addr · s1 len · s2 accumulator · s3 index · s5 the current byte.
# The loop pushes a pessimistic stop flag 1 first and the digit branch replaces
# it, so both paths leave exactly one cell for `}` to pop; the byte goes through
# s5 rather than staying on the stack for the same reason.  Accumulation is
# `acc ﹡10 ﹢ digit` in 64-bit wraparound arithmetic: no overflow is detected,
# 18446744073709551615 is the largest value that round-trips, and one more digit
# silently wraps.  `i` is a leaf, so calling it inside the frame is free.
:n X5177448\store 5177440\store 0 5177456\store 0 5177464\store 5177448\fetch 0\eq[{1 5177440\fetch 5177464\fetch\radd?5177480\store 5177480\fetch i[5177480\fetch 48\rsub 5177456\fetch 10\rmul\radd 5177456\store 5177464\fetch 1\radd 5177464\store _5177464\fetch 5177448\fetch\rlt 1\radd]}]5177456\fetch 5177464\fetch Y;

# o : write v as unsigned decimal at addr, and return how many bytes that took.
# The prelude's `Ṅ` prints straight to fd 1, which is useless to a tool that
# owns its output stream and must interleave the digits with everything else —
# and `Ṅ` is SIGNED besides, so it renders 2^64-1 as "-1".  Digits come out
# least-significant first but have to land most-significant first, so the word
# counts them (s2) and then fills BACKWARDS from that count (s3 walks down).
# s0 v · s1 addr · s2 digit count · s3 scratch quotient, then the write index.
# The counting loop is guarded on v/10 so that v < 10 yields exactly one digit;
# the emit loop is not guarded, because one digit always has to come out — 0 is
# the value that a "while v != 0" loop would print as the empty string.
# `o` WRITES, and takes no room argument: size the destination for the worst
# case, which is 20 bytes (18446744073709551615).  It writes exactly the count
# it returns and never a terminator.
:o X5177448\store 5177440\store 5177440\fetch 10/5177464\store 1 5177456\store 5177464\fetch 0\eq[{5177456\fetch 1\radd 5177456\store 5177464\fetch 10/5177464\store 5177464\fetch 0\eq}]5177456\fetch 5177464\store{5177464\fetch 1\rsub 5177464\store 5177440\fetch 10%48\radd 5177448\fetch 5177464\fetch\radd,5177440\fetch 10/5177440\store 5177464\fetch 0\eq}5177456\fetch Y;

# p : does the range (a,alen) start with the range (b,blen)?  0 yes, 1 no.
# Also the equality test — `<a> <n> <b> <n> p` — see the header.  A needle
# longer than the haystack is 1 without touching memory, which is exactly what
# makes this safe to sweep along a buffer to its very last byte.  An EMPTY
# needle is 0, matching Python's `"abc".startswith("")`; `r` depends on it.
# s0 a · s1 alen · s2 b · s3 blen · s4 result · s5 index.  The per-byte compare
# writes its verdict straight into s4, so the surviving value after the loop is
# the verdict of the byte that stopped it: on a mismatch, 1; on running out of
# needle, the last (matching) 0.  The continue flag is `s4 ⊔ (i >= blen)`.
:p X5177464\store 5177456\store 5177448\store 5177440\store 1 5177472\store 5177464\fetch 5177448\fetch 1\radd\rlt 1\radd[0 5177472\store 0 5177480\store 5177464\fetch 0\eq[{5177440\fetch 5177480\fetch\radd?5177456\fetch 5177480\fetch\radd?\eq 1\radd 5177472\store 5177480\fetch 1\radd 5177480\store 5177472\fetch 5177480\fetch 5177464\fetch\rlt 1\radd\rmax}]]5177472\fetch Y;

# q : index of the first ch in (addr,len), or -1.  The workhorse: splitting
# data/blob.hex into lines and data/codepage.tsv into fields is this word twice.
# s0 addr · s1 len · s2 ch · s3 index · s4 result · s5 hit flag.  The result
# starts at -1 and is only ever written on a hit, so an empty range or a byte
# that is not there falls straight through the guard with -1 intact.
# NOTE THE FLAG FLIP.  s5 is zero-is-true — 0 MEANS a hit — which is what `[`
# wants, but the exact opposite of what `}` wants: `}` loops on 0, so the stop
# flag has to be 1 on a hit.  Hence `1 s5 ﹣` before the `⊔` with (i >= len);
# feeding s5 in raw stops the sweep at the first byte that is NOT the one being
# looked for, which is a search that only ever finds things at index 0.
:q X5177456\store 5177448\store 5177440\store 1\neg 5177472\store 0 5177464\store 5177448\fetch 0\eq[{5177440\fetch 5177464\fetch\radd?5177456\fetch\eq 1\radd 5177480\store 5177480\fetch[5177464\fetch 5177472\store]5177464\fetch 1\radd 5177464\store 1 5177480\fetch\rsub 5177464\fetch 5177448\fetch\rlt 1\radd\rmax}]5177472\fetch Y;

# r : index of the first occurrence of (b,blen) inside (a,alen), or -1.  Naive
# O(alen*blen) sweep — the needles here are mnemonics and field separators, a
# handful of bytes each, and a smarter search would cost more code than the
# whole library saves.  s0 a · s1 alen · s2 b · s3 blen · s4 result · s5 hit ·
# s6 position · s7 the number of positions to try, alen-blen+1.  `p` frames, so
# it may be called from inside this loop without disturbing any of that; each
# call is handed the REMAINING haystack (alen ﹣ i), which is always at least
# blen because i stops at alen-blen.  A needle longer than the haystack skips
# the loop entirely and yields -1; an empty needle yields 0, since p says yes at
# position 0 — both match Python's `bytes.find`.  s5 carries `p`'s zero-is-true
# verdict and is flipped with `1 s5 ﹣` for the stop flag, exactly as in `q` and
# for the same reason: `[` runs on 0, `}` loops on 0, and they want opposites.
:r X5177464\store 5177456\store 5177448\store 5177440\store 1\neg 5177472\store 0 5177488\store 5177464\fetch 5177448\fetch 1\radd\rlt 1\radd[5177448\fetch 5177464\fetch\rsub 1\radd 5177496\store{5177440\fetch 5177488\fetch\radd 5177448\fetch 5177488\fetch\rsub 5177456\fetch 5177464\fetch p 5177480\store 5177480\fetch[5177488\fetch 5177472\store]5177488\fetch 1\radd 5177488\store 1 5177480\fetch\rsub 5177488\fetch 5177496\fetch\rlt 1\radd\rmax}]5177472\fetch Y;
